et=os.readlink(p)\n  if target\"'!=e.get('\"'target'): mismatch.append((path,'symlink-target',e.get('target'),target))\nextra=set(oe)-set(ce); print('extra_entries',sorted(extra)[:20], 'count',len(extra)); print('nonmetadata_mismatches',len(mismatch));\nfor m in mismatch[:100]: print('MISMATCH',m)\nprint('allowed_metadata_deltas',allowed)\nPY\necho \\\"\"'$?\" > \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/integrity-check.exit\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/integrity-check.stdout\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/integrity-check.stderr\";'","aggregated_output":"canonical_manifest_sha256 1bc0f46ef4ad58e9f70bb654d761eb330f15d1c87929ad165d414907dd7d0f68\noperational_manifest_sha256 1bc0f46ef4ad58e9f70bb654d761eb330f15d1c87929ad165d414907dd7d0f68\nmanifests_equal True\ncanonical_entries 1140 operational_entries 1140 path_sets_equal True\nextra_entries [] count 0\nnonmetadata_mismatches 0\nallowed_metadata_deltas [('content/workspace/.git/annex/keysdb/db', 'sha256', 'f6f8fd7af99434db59fadc6da021051789c6aaa271ebf878c54dafc6c9fde5ca', '233c0be3e02857e0fbf47b00b8a94de3e9126529bee41b2491286aef7372be80')]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\" | head -60; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/response.json\" | head -n 10;'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\" | head -60; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/response.json\" | head -n 10;'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Potential gains and brain activity\n     2\t\n     3\t## Conclusion\n     4\t\n     5\tThe supplied equal-indifference group maps do **not support** the prediction of a positive vmPFC gain effect under whole-brain family-wise error control. No significant whole-brain voxel overlapped the prespecified anatomical vmPFC proxy (9 voxels survived whole-brain correction elsewhere). The proxy's mean participant-level gain-effect estimate was near zero, with a confidence interval spanning zero. This is a failure to find corrected evidence for the prediction, not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Group analysis\n     8\t\n     9\tI analyzed all 54 equal-indifference participants with their unthresholded AO86 gain-effect COPE maps. The group model was a one-sample, voxelwise t test against zero (df=53), testing the predicted positive direction. The common analysis mask contained 210,968 voxels (nonzero coverage in at least 90% of maps). All maps matched the source manifest's byte counts and SHA-256 checksums, shared a voxel grid and affine, and contained only finite values; no participant was excluded.\n    10\t\n    11\tTo control family-wise error across the entire mask, I used Bonferroni correction over all 210,968 voxels at alpha=.05. This is conservative but controls FWE without relying on spatial-independence assumptions. Nine positive voxels survived (peak t(53)=6.62, uncorrected p=9.31×10⁻⁹; Bonferroni p=.00196; MNI x=-26, y=-102, z=-3.6 mm). They were in posterior/inferior locations, not the hypothesized vmPFC. The full corrected voxel table is in [`results/fwe_positive_voxels.csv`](results/fwe_positive_voxels.csv).\n    12\t\n    13\tI operationalized vmPFC using the Harvard–Oxford cortical atlas: Frontal Medial, Subcallosal, and Frontal Orbital parcels, limited to |MNI x|≤24 mm and z≤10 mm, resampled to the supplied map grid with nearest-neighbor interpolation. This broad atlas-derived mask is an anatomical proxy, not a unique definition of vmPFC; the atlas provides structural parcels rather than a functional boundary ([FSL atlas documentation](https://fsl.fmrib.ox.ac.uk/fsl/docs/other/datasets.html)). No corrected voxel fell in this proxy. As a descriptive secondary summary, the participant-average COPE in the proxy was 0.0002 (SD across participants=0.0816; 95% t interval [-0.0221, 0.0225]; t(53)=0.018, one-sided uncorrected p=.493). Leaving out one participant at a time moved the proxy mean between -0.0041 and 0.0030, so its sign was not robust to individual omission. This ROI summary is not the basis for the whole-brain decision.\n    14\t\n    15\t![Mean gain effect and whole-brain corrected positive voxels](figures/whole_brain_results.png)\n    16\t\n    17\t![Participant-level vmPFC proxy estimates](figures/vmPFC_proxy_participants.png)\n    18\t\n    19\t## Interpretation and limits\n    20\t\n    21\tThe supplied values are parameter estimates, not t or z statistics. AO86 mean-centered and variance-normalized the gain regressor before HRF convolution, so this contrast is not a response-per-currency-unit slope and cannot be converted to a monetary gain increment. It is conditional on AO86's separate gain/loss model and response-time-duration choice regressors. The group t model uses between-participant variability in these COPEs; participant variance maps were unavailable, so it cannot incorporate first-level uncertainty using a full weighted mixed-effects model. The results therefore concern the supplied model's group-average gain effect, not a model-independent causal response to cash amounts.\n    22\t\n    23\tThe 95% interval is compatible with small effects in either direction in the COPE scale. A null result after stringent whole-brain correction does not establish no vmPFC response; it means this analysis did not find sufficient corrected evidence that the mean effect is positive. The atlas proxy's borders and template differ slightly from the supplied MNI152NLin2009cAsym images, adding localization uncertainty. Bonferroni's conservatism also reduces sensitivity. The original NARPS instructions required whole-brain-corrected results for the binary hypothesis decision ([NARPS analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)).\n    24\t\n    25\t## Reproducibility\n    26\t\n    27\tThe initial workspace had 54 broken Git-annex pointers in `input/data/`, without accessible image bytes. I restored only the 54 exact COPE maps referenced by `input/sources.json` from its NeuroVault URLs and verified each downloaded file against the recorded size and SHA-256. No time-series scans were retrieved. To reproduce with the provided Python 3.13 environment:\n    28\t\n    29\t```bash\n    30\tpython analysis/restore_maps.py\n    31\tpython analysis/group_analysis.py\n    32\t```\n    33\t\n    34\tThe group script writes the statistical maps and summary to `results/`, the voxel and participant QC tables there, and the figures to `figures/`. Key numeric results are in [`results/summary.json`](results/summary.json); per-map checks are in [`results/map_qc.csv`](results/map_qc.csv). Analysis code is [`analysis/group_analysis.py`](analysis/group_analysis.py), and the map restoration/checksum code is [`analysis/restore_maps.py`](analysis/restore_maps.py).\n{\n  \"checklist_version\": \"0.3.1\",\n  \"responses\": {\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/001\": {\n      \"value\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"not yet checked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"source\": \"judgement\",\n      \"flags\": []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"The evidence package matches the canonical capture at the file level. My read-only Git/probe inspection did change the annex keys database file; that is within the separately classified operational-metadata exception, but I will report the delta without claiming logical database equivalence. The scientific report’s corrected whole-brain result is interpretable and appropriately limits its conclusion to lack of corrected support."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat > \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/assessment-report.md\" <<'\"'EOF'\n# Independent assessment\n\n## Identity and scope\n\nAssessed the complete operational copy against canonical capture SHA-256 \"'`1bc0f46ef4ad58e9f70bb654d761eb330f15d1c87929ad165d414907dd7d0f68`; captured HEAD is `675620fed8a134102b1b07226fe39d67630ee6f7`. The worker stop receipt identifies the same capture and HEAD and reports a complete capture with no capture errors or omissions (receipt SHA-256 `17bbce762c9dc4b3e7c27f16479e30072174e2fd5dac626ab71bf147ee863588`). Canonical and operational manifest bytes are identical. A post-inspection comparison found no non-metadata differences among 1,140 workspace entries. `.git/annex/keysdb/db` changed from SHA-256 `f6f8fd7af99434db59fadc6da021051789c6aaa271ebf878c54dafc6c9fde5ca` to `233c0be3e02857e0fbf47b00b8a94de3e9126529bee41b2491286aef7372be80`; this is separately classified operational metadata and is consistent with inspection-induced annex activity, not worker evidence. Logical database equivalence is not established.\n\nAll citations to target artifacts below use the canonical capture SHA above and capture-relative paths. The worker report is `content/workspace/report.md` (SHA-256 `1ea0a99f038b149f8b8dc1e6ddfc0d15ba85e33bda53b9cb4198fb5cb1475849`); the main analysis is `content/workspace/analysis/group_analysis.py` (SHA-256 `6a5399ab6457465ef8c165fc90b717f8f635ecaa764b4edd58b707f838129776`); numeric summary is `content/workspace/results/summary.json` (SHA-256 `319492c13da18cfa6c3ce1bbc2629b5fdd0a494209588167d8baca248b11a8ec`). The full frozen probe output is in `probes/probes.json`; probe results informed but did not replace these judgments.\n\n## Scientific assessment\n\n**Scientific adequacy: supported.** The report supports its bounded conclusion that these supplied group maps did not provide whole-brain-corrected evidence for a positive vmPFC gain effect. It correctly keeps that outcome distinct from evidence that the true response is zero. The analysis uses 54 equal-indifference participants, unthresholded participant COPEs, a positive one-sample voxelwise t test (df=53), and Bonferroni family-wise correction across 210,968 voxels. Nine positive voxels survived, all reported outside the vmPFC proxy; the proxy average was near zero with a 95% interval spanning zero. These methods address the specified group-stage hypothesis and the original whole-brain correction requirement. The reported outcome is **not supported** (no corrected vmPFC positive finding), which is not the same as scientific inadequacy.\n\nEvidence: `content/workspace/report.md` lines 9–13 and 21–23; `content/workspace/analysis/group_analysis.py` (model, mask, correction, ROI and QC calculations); `content/workspace/results/summary.json`; and `content/workspace/results/fwe_positive_voxels.csv` (all nine corrected coordinates, each marked outside the proxy). The methods appropriately treat COPEs as parameter estimates, not monetary-unit slopes; note the separate gain/loss upstream model and missing first-level variance maps; and limit inference to the supplied upstream model. The atlas-derived vmPFC definition is defensible and described as a proxy. Bonferroni is conservative but valid for whole-brain family-wise control.\n\nLimitations qualify the scope, not the reported no-support conclusion: the mask includes voxels nonzero in at least 90% of maps, so zero values in the remaining maps enter as observations rather than being modeled as missing; the report does not separately characterize that small amount of coverage variation. The Harvard–Oxford atlas is fetched at runtime rather than retained and pinned, which limits exact reproduction of the ROI boundary. No first-level variance maps are available. These limits argue against interpreting the result as proof of no vmPFC response or a response-per-currency-unit effect. No replay was performed, so the retained outputs were assessed as supplied results, not as replay evidence.\n\n## STAMPED evidence and attainment\n\nThe exact stopped workspace contains the report, scripts, figures, statistical maps, tables and all 54 gain-effect maps. The report describes a two-script entrypoint and its generated results. `input/sources.json` and `analysis/restore_maps.py` provide a SHA-256 and byte-count checked retrieval route for the participant maps. The outputs and source maps are present in the captured delivery, but at worker stop the analysis scripts, report, figures and result files were untracked; the 54 annex-backed paths were changed from tracked symlinks to materialized regular files. The inspection-time Git status on the operational copy reports these paths as untracked or type-changed. The captured HEAD commit message is `chore(study): supply materialized scientific inputs`; in keeping with the stop receipt and attribution rules, that commit and inherited annex objects are not credited as worker-authored versioning or annex publication. The worker stop receipt is the authority for stop-state attribution; the operational status is explicitly an assessor observation.\n\nThe frozen probes found no tracked environment specification, license or CI configuration; their automated entry-point and clean-tree findings were supplemented by reading the report and scripts in the complete stopped delivery. Checklist item verdicts and evidence are recorded individually in `response.json`; not-checked is retained where the evidence does not establish a claim. No overall score is assigned.\n\nAn exposure is recorded: the captured workspace includes `.entire` session/log metadata and `.codex` configuration, which indicate agent-assisted tooling. These records were not used to grade the scientific work or checklist attainment. No treatment assignment was inferred or reported.\n\n## Access and replay\n\n**Recipient and tested route:** a researcher who has the worker’s report/scripts and can use the declared route can retrieve the 54 participant COPE maps using `analysis/restore_maps.py` and `input/sources.json`, which point to NeuroVault and validate each expected byte count and SHA-256. HTTP HEAD checks to all 54 declared map URLs succeeded at assessment time (host scope: `neurovault.org`); no payload was fetched during the access check. This tests availability of the map artifacts only. No worker-declared route was found that publishes or provides access to the complete report, code, results and environment as a research object. Coordinator delivery to the assessor is not evidence that the worker had published the complete object. The detailed access record is `access-record.json`.\n\n**Replay: not checked.** The report documents `python analysis/restore_maps.py` followed by `python analysis/group_analysis.py`; the latter generates the outputs named in its code under `results/` and `figures/`. However, the worker supplied no dependency/environment declaration. The analysis imports NumPy, pandas, nibabel, SciPy, nilearn, Matplotlib and Pillow and fetches an atlas at runtime. The report’s mention of Python 3.13 does not specify or pin these dependencies or the atlas resource. Under the scope rule, I did not invent an environment or run the code with assessor-installed dependencies. No replay attempt was made, no shipped outputs are treated as replay evidence, and no target files were altered. `replay-record.json` records the reason and the documented commands/output selection.\n\n## Item-level summary\n\n- **Met:** must/001, must/002, must/008, must/009, should/003, may/002, may/003, may/004, may/005, should/001.\n- **Unmet:** must/003, must/004, must/005, must/006, must/007, must/010–017, should/005, should/007, should/008.\n- **Not checked:** should/002, should/004, should/006.\n- **Not applicable:** should/009, may/001.\n\nThe complete response JSON provides each item’s rationale and capture-cited evidence. The generated checklist URL is in `checklist-link.txt`.\nEOF\ncat > \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/replay-record.json\" <<'\"'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"not-checked\\\",\n  \\\"attempts\\\": 0,\n  \\\"maximum_attempts\\\": 1,\n  \\\"execution_limit_seconds\\\": 7200,\n  \\\"source\\\": \\\"canonical original complete delivery\\\",\n  \\\"documented_entrypoint\\\": [\\\"python analysis/restore_maps.py\\\", \\\"python analysis/group_analysis.py\\\"],\n  \\\"prerequisites\\\": {\n    \\\"documented\\\": [\\\"Python 3.13 (report.md)\\\"],\n    \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"dependency specification or lock\\\", \\\"pinned/captured Harvard-Oxford atlas resource\\\"],\n    \\\"reason\\\": \\\"The worker did not supply a dependency declaration. The group script imports NumPy, pandas, nibabel, SciPy, nilearn, Matplotlib and Pillow and fetches an atlas at runtime. Reconstructing those from assessor environment state would exceed the supplied instructions/declarations.\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"scientific_output_selection\\\": [\n    \\\"results/map_qc.csv\\\",\n    \\\"results/group_mean_cope.nii.gz\\\",\n    \\\"results/group_t_positive.nii.gz\\\",\n    \\\"results/group_neglog10_p_bonferroni.nii.gz\\\",\n    \\\"results/summary.json\\\",\n    \\\"results/fwe_positive_voxels.csv\\\",\n    \\\"results/vmPFC_proxy_participant_estimates.csv\\\",\n    \\\"figures/group_mean_cope.png\\\",\n    \\\"figures/whole_brain_fwe.png\\\",\n    \\\"figures/whole_brain_results.png\\\",\n    \\\"figures/vmPFC_proxy_participants.png\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"comparison_rule_if_run\\\": \\\"Compare reported scientific numeric quantities and map values at rtol=1e-6; separate timestamps, logs, prose, formatting, atlas/version changes and stochastic/numerical differences. No replay was attempted, so no comparison was made.\\\",\n  \\\"input_protection\\\": \\\"No replay copy was created because required environment reconstruction could not be established. No source inputs or target files were changed by a replay.\\\",\n  \\\"attempts_record\\\": []\n}\nEOF\ncat > \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/access-record.json\" <<'\"'EOF'\nEOF\ncp \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/access-record.json\" \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/access-record.json.tmp\"\n'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
